\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Strikes Called at Dozens of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Locations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers are pressing for a contract and accuse the company of anti-labor tactics. Management blames the union for the lack of bargaining progress.\par \par \uc2 The union organizing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers declared a strike at dozens of stores on Friday, the latest escalation in its campaign to secure a labor contract.\par \par \uc2 The strike is intended to last for three days at many of the stores, according to Workers United, the union representing the workers. It follows a one-day strike at roughly 100 stores last month.\par \par \uc2 Workers involved in the strike say they are protesting the company's slow pace of bargaining and its recent closing of unionized stores, including one near Broadway and Denny Way in Seattle. They also complain about understaffing and cuts to their hours.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  sent a clear message when they closed the Broadway and Denny store,'' Michelle Eisen, a Buffalo-based barista who has been a leader of the union campaign, said in a statement. ''They're doubling down on their union-busting, so we're doubling down, too. We're demanding fair staffing, an end to store closures, and that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  bargain with us in good faith.''\par \par \uc2 Several \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers at a store in Chicago stood outside in snowy conditions holding picket signs, but the store was open, and a manager said he was preparing orders for pickup. The company said many stores where workers were striking remained open.\par \par \uc2 The union, which represents workers at more than 250 of the roughly 9,000 company-owned stores in the United States, said \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  representatives had walked out of dozens of bargaining sessions this fall shortly after they began, preventing progress on a contract.\par \par \uc2 The company said it closed the Seattle store along with more than 35 others nationwide since July, typically because of security concerns, including about 10 unionized stores.\par \par \uc2 It said that its negotiators had walked out of bargaining sessions because union representatives sought to broadcast the sessions to co-workers outside the room using video-chat software, potentially inhibiting the discussions, but that negotiators had ''come to the table time after time prepared to bargain in good faith.''\par \par \uc2 The union won its first vote at a store in Buffalo last December, and the campaign spread quickly across the country during the first half of this year.\par \par \uc2 But organizing has slowed in recent months, with filings for union elections dropping from about 70 in March to fewer than 10 in August.\par \par \uc2 Workers United has argued that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  impeded the organizing campaign by firing dozens of union supporters and excluding unionized stores from recent wage increases and new benefits.\par \par \uc2 The federal labor board has issued numerous complaints over these accusations, and judges at the agency have ruled against the company in some cases. The company has appealed at least one of the rulings.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has said that the firings reflect violations of company policies and that it is legally prohibited from granting new benefits and wage increases at unionized stores without bargaining over them first.\par \par \uc2 Workers in St. Paul, Minn. The strike is to last at least three days at many Starbucks, the union said. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KEREM Y\u220\'00\'DCCEL/MINNESOTA PUBLIC RADIO, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221217eich0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\