\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 New York Times Union Holds One-Day Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By The New York Times \par \uc2 782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Negotiators for the company and the union, which represents most of the newsroom, have failed to come to an agreement on salaries, health and retirement benefits, and other issues.\par \par \uc2 Reporters and editors at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  began a one-day strike on Thursday, saying talks between their union and the company had dragged on and showed limited progress.\par \par \uc2 The contract between The Times and The New York Times Guild expired in March 2021, and about 40 bargaining sessions have been held since. Negotiators have failed to come to an agreement on salaries, health and retirement benefits, and other issues.\par \par \uc2 More than 1,100 employees signed a pledge to strike for 24 hours. The union negotiating the contract, which is part of the NewsGuild of New York, represents about 1,450 employees in the newsroom, advertising and other areas of the company. More than 1,800 people work in The Times's newsroom.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Wednesday evening, the union accused The Times of bargaining in bad faith.\par \par \uc2 ''Their wage proposal still fails to meet the economic moment, lagging far behind both inflation and the average rate of wage gains in the U.S.,'' the union said in its announcement that it would strike.\par \par \uc2 In a note to the newsroom, \uc2 Joe Kahn\uc2 , the executive editor of The Times, said he was disappointed with the union's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''Strikes typically happen when talks deadlock. That is not where we are today,'' Mr. Kahn said. ''While the company and the NewsGuild remain apart on a number of issues, we continue to trade proposals and make progress toward an agreement.''\par \par \uc2 Compensation remains the most contentious aspect of the negotiations. The Times has offered union members a 5.5 percent raise upon ratification of the contract, 3 percent raises in 2023 and 2024, and a 4 percent retroactive bonus to compensate for a lack of raises since the contract expired. The union has proposed a 10 percent raise upon ratification, 5.5 percent raises in 2023 and 2024, and an 8.5 percent retroactive bonus.\par \par \uc2 Other issues discussed during talks include return-to-work policies and the company's performance rating system for employees. In a study it released in August, the union said the system was discriminatory.\par \par \uc2 ''White Guild members were more likely to get the top ratings,'' the study stated, ''while Black and Hispanic members were more likely to get the lowest two ratings.''\par \par \uc2 After the union released that report, a team of senior managers at The Times studied ways to improve the rating process. In October, Marc Lacey, a managing editor, announced plans to update it.\par \par \uc2 ''Our ultimate aim is to have a simpler system that everyone applies fairly and consistently, and to focus more on thoughtful feedback than ratings,'' Mr. Lacey wrote.\par \par \uc2 During the walkout, nonunion newsroom employees will largely be responsible for producing the news report.\par \par \uc2 ''We will produce a robust report on Thursday,'' Mr. Kahn wrote in his email to the newsroom. ''But it will be harder than usual.''\par \par \uc2 Facing a slowdown in advertising and an uncertain economic outlook, some media organizations have cut staff in recent weeks, including CNN, \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  and the Gannett newspaper chain. During its negotiations with The Times, union negotiators have argued that reporters and editors are struggling with inflation as the company produces healthy operating profits.\par \par \uc2 Meredith Kopit Levien\uc2 , chief executive of The Times, wrote in a companywide email that investments in the news report had resulted in high-paying, secure jobs for many journalists. She said that profits had not caught up to where they were decades ago.\par \par \uc2 ''Those investments are possible because of the great care we've taken as a company over the last decade to work our way back to economic growth in a radically transforming industry,'' Ms. Levien said. ''We've done so by making financial decisions that are sustainable not just in the moment, but for years to come.''\par \par \uc2 Times journalists have rarely gone on strike. They did so for less than a day in 1981, and there was a brief walkout in 2017 to protest the elimination of the copy desk. No labor action has stopped publication of The Times since a strike of pressmen and others in 1978, which lasted 88 days.\par \par \uc2 The union demonstrated outside The Times's headquarters in New York on Thursday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Members of the New York Times Guild protested at the company's Manhattan headquarters on Thursday. More than 1,100 employees signed a pledge to strike. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABRIELA BHASKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221209eic900035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
