\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Future in Big Tech Dims For Computing Students\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natasha Singer and Kalley Huang \par \uc2 1,598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new reality is setting in for students and recent graduates who spent years honing themselves for careers at the largest tech companies.\par \par \uc2 Ever since she was a 10th grader in Seattle, Annalice Ni wanted to develop software for a prominent tech company like \uc2 Google\uc2 . So she went to great lengths to meet the internship and other r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 criteria that make students attractive hires to the biggest tech firms.\par \par \uc2 In high school, Ms. Ni took computer science courses, interned at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and volunteered as a coding teacher for younger students. She majored in computer science at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 , earning coveted software engineering internships at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . After graduating from college this year, she moved to Silicon Valley to start her dream job as a software engineer at \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's parent company.\par \par \uc2 Then last month, \uc2 Meta\uc2  laid off more than 11,000 employees -- including Ms. Ni.\par \par \uc2 ''I did feel very frustrated and disappointed and maybe a bit scared because all of a sudden, I didn't know what to do,'' Ms. Ni, 22, said of her unexpected career setback. ''There's not much I could have done, especially in college, more than I already did, better than I already did.''\par \par \uc2 Over the last decade, the prospect of six-figure starting salaries, perks like free food and the chance to work on apps used by billions led young people to stampede toward computer science -- the study of computer programming and processes like algorithms -- on college campuses across the United States. The number of undergraduates majoring in the subject more than tripled from 2011 to 2021, to nearly 136,000 students, according to the \uc2 Computing Research Association\uc2 , which tracks computing degrees at about 200 universities.\par \par \uc2 Tech giants like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  encouraged the computing education boom, promoting software jobs to students as a route to lucrative careers and the power to change the world.\par \par \uc2 But now, layoffs, hiring freezes and planned recruiting slowdowns at \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , Snap and \uc2 Stripe\uc2  are sending shock waves through a generation of computer and data science students who spent years honing themselves for careers at the largest tech companies. Tech executives have blamed a faltering global economy for the jobs slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The cutbacks have not only sent recent graduates scrambling to find new jobs but also created uncertainty for college students seeking high-paying summer internships at large consumer tech companies.\par \par \uc2 In the past, tech companies used their internship programs to recruit promising job candidates, extending offers to many students to return as full-time employees after graduation. But this year, those opportunities are shrinking.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , for instance, hired about 18,000 interns this year, paying some computer science students nearly $30,000 for the summer, not including housing stipends. The company is now considering reducing the number of interns for 2023 by more than half, said a person with knowledge of the program who was not authorized to speak publicly.\par \par \uc2 Brad Glasser, an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesman, said the company was committed to its internship program and the real-world experience that it provided. A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokeswoman referred to a letter to employees from \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , the company's chief executive, announcing the company's layoffs last month.\par \par \uc2 Hiring plans are also changing at smaller tech firms. \uc2 Roblox\uc2 , the popular game platform, said it planned to hire 300 interns for next summer -- almost twice as many as this year -- and was expecting more than 50,000 applications for those spots. \uc2 Redfin\uc2 , which employed 38 interns this summer, said it had canceled the program for next year.\par \par \uc2 There are still good jobs for computing students, and the field is growing. Between 2021 and 2031, employment for software developers and testers is expected to grow 25 percent, amounting to more than 411,000 new jobs, according to projections from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . But many of those jobs are in areas like finance and the automotive industry.\par \par \uc2 ''Students are still getting multiple job offers,'' said Brent Winkelman, chief of staff for the computer science department at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 . ''They just may not come from \uc2 Meta\uc2 , from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . They're going to come from places like G.M., \uc2 Toyota\uc2  or \uc2 Lockheed\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 College career centers have become sounding boards for anxious students on the cusp of entering the tech job market. In career counselors' offices, the search for a Plan B has heightened.\par \par \uc2 Some students are applying to lesser-known tech companies. Others are seeking tech jobs outside the industry, with retailers like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  or with government agencies and nonprofits. Graduate school is also an option.\par \par \uc2 ''This particular class has been a lot more savvy than previous classes,'' said Hazel Raja, senior director of the career development office at Pomona College in Claremont, Calif. ''Even those who have secured job offers, they're still making sure they're networking and staying engaged in campus recruiting opportunities.''\par \par \uc2 Helen Dong, 21, a senior majoring in computer science at \uc2 Carnegie Mellon University\uc2 , interned at \uc2 Meta\uc2  twice, in 2021 and 2022. So she was surprised at the end of this summer, she said, when she did not receive a job offer from the company. \uc2 Meta\uc2 's recent layoffs prompted her to apply for jobs outside tech, at automotive and financial companies. Last month, she posted videos on TikTok advising her peers to adjust their job expectations.\par \par \uc2 ''I chose to major in computer science so that I could get a ton of offers after college and make bank,'' Ms. Dong joked in one TikTok, as she sang along to ''Reduce Your Expectations to 0.'' In this job market, she wrote at the bottom of the video, ''be grateful with 1 offer.''\par \par \uc2 In interviews, 10 college students and recent graduates said they were not prepared for a slowdown in jobs at the largest tech companies. Until recently, those companies were fiercely competing to hire computer science majors at top schools -- with some students receiving multiple job offers with six-figure starting salaries and five-digit signing bonuses. An entire genre of TikTok videos had sprung up dedicated to young techies extolling their job perks and their annual compensation, with at least one highlighting a $198,000 package, complete with stock options and relocation expenses.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of people who were recently laid off, or whose tech job offers were rescinded, have posted details of their plights on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . To alert recruiters, some have added the hashtag #opentowork to their \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profile photos.\par \par \uc2 Tony Shi, 23, who majored in computer science and business at \uc2 Western University in London\uc2 , Ontario, is one of them. After graduating this year, he began working as a product manager at \uc2 Lyft\uc2  in August. In November, the ride-hailing company laid off about 650 employees, including Mr. Shi.\par \par \uc2 Now he is on a tight deadline to find a new job. Mr. Shi is Canadian, from Waterloo, Ontario, and obtained a visa to move to San Francisco for his job at \uc2 Lyft\uc2 . Under the visa, he has 60 days to find a new job. He said he had become more sensitive to the businesses and balance sheets of potential employers.\par \par \uc2 ''I need to be a little more risk-averse. I definitely don't want to get laid off again,'' he said. Instead of his taking a company for its word, he added, ''now, the product needs to make a lot of sense.''\par \par \uc2 Some recent graduates did not get the chance to start their new tech jobs.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Castellino, a statistics major at the California Polytechnic State University, worked to land a job at a major tech company. During college, she interned as a project manager at \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , received a data science fellowship funded by the \uc2 National Science Foundation\uc2  and founded a data science club at her school.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Castellino, 22, knew she would have to grind to pass companies' technical interviews, which typically involve solving programming problems. Last year, she spent much of the fall job hunting and preparing for coding assessments. For four days a week, from 8 a.m. to 4 p.m., she studied probability concepts and programming languages. Even so, she said, the interview process was brutal.\par \par \uc2 In November 2021, \uc2 Meta\uc2  offered her a job as a data scientist, starting in December 2022. Last month, \uc2 Meta\uc2  rescinded the offer, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''I worked so hard for those interviews. It felt really good to earn something of a high caliber,'' she said. ''I had so much to look forward to.''\par \par \uc2 The setback has been disheartening. ''I was upset,'' Ms. Castellino said. ''It wasn't good to hear.''\par \par \uc2 As for Ms. Ni, she now views losing her dream job as an opportunity to broaden her career horizons. Over the last month, she has applied to midsize tech firms and start-ups that she finds innovative -- potential employers she had not previously considered.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm exploring opportunities that I didn't before,'' Ms. Ni said. ''I feel like I've already learned some things.''\par \par \uc2 Karen Weise contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Karen Weise contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Annalice Ni, 22, above, was one of the 11,000 employees who were laid off by Meta last month. (B1); Meta rescinded its job offer to Rachel Castellino, 22, above, weeks before she was scheduled to start work. ''I had so much to look forward to,'' she said. Annalice Ni spends her days interviewing and brushing up on her skills, right. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JASON HENRY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B5) This article appeared in print on page B1, B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221208eic800033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
