\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Rail Deal Avoids Crisis and Opens Pitfalls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Prasad Philbrick \par \uc2 1,871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Congress stepped in to prevent supply chain havoc, but rail workers' biggest grievance remains unaddressed.\par \par \uc2 Congress and President Biden imposed a labor agreement between major railroad companies and their workers last week, averting the possibility of a strike that would have disrupted the economy in the middle of the holiday shopping season. The agreement gives rail workers a pay raise and other benefits but not paid medical leave. I spoke to my colleague Peter S. Goodman, who covers supply chains, about what's behind the workers' discontent.\par \par \uc2 Ian: The deal that Congress enforced is one that the Biden administration helped negotiate earlier this year , which several unions rejected. Why were they against it?\par \par \uc2 Peter: The lack of paid sick leave caused the workers I spoke with to vote it down, that and draconian scheduling policies. Rail workers are constantly missing wedding anniversaries, funerals, birthday parties. It's baked into the job. But in addition to that, there's pressure to be at the job site even when they've got emergencies or sick children.\par \par \uc2 Here's an example. I talked to a guy named Anthony Gunter, who's based in eastern Tennessee and worked on maintenance crews repairing tracks for \uc2 Norfolk Southern Railway\uc2 . His dad had worked there for 40 years, and Gunter remembers trying to sneak into his duffel bag as a kid to join him on the road. Gunter regularly worked four 10-hour shifts in a row, swinging giant hammers, pounding stakes into railroad ties. His son had been born with a heart defect, and last year he stayed home for his son's surgery. His supervisor pressured him to come back, saying: ''You're putting me in a tough spot. You have to be here.'' Gunter was furious, so he quit.\par \par \uc2 Wow. That sounds like a difficult choice. What about workers who ended up supporting the deal?\par \par \uc2 There was unhappiness even among workers whose unions voted to ratify it. But their calculus was: Let's be pragmatic. There's no way in hell they're going to let us strike, Congress is going to intervene and this is the best we're going to get.\par \par \uc2 The deal did benefit workers on the issue of reimbursements for lodging on the road. Many rail workers spend long periods away from their families, with schedules subject to change. Maintenance gang workers like Gunter, who sometimes drove 12 hours from his home, have traditionally been given reimbursement rates so low that they eat terribly and stay two or three to a room in crappy motels. One worker told me he buys cheap clothes to sleep in and throws them away because he's scared of bringing bedbugs home to his family. So higher reimbursement rates are a victory.\par \par \uc2 Have working conditions always been this bad for rail workers?\par \par \uc2 From the beginning, in the 19th century, the railroads were run by financiers who operated them as financial assets, often to the detriment of service. \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 , one of the companies that built the Transcontinental Railroad, made a priority of securing land from the federal government instead of creating efficient routes. Another company pressed Chinese laborers into service to build the tracks to drive down wages. So railroads have always employed fairly ruthless techniques to keep a lid on costs while rewarding investors.\par \par \uc2 And you can argue that what they've done in recent years is about gratifying Wall Street. They laid off nearly a third of their work force before the pandemic, worsening freight service while increasing profits, and handed out handsome stock dividends. It's good for shareholders. It's good for investors. But shippers have complained, and it's miserable for workers because there are fewer people to do the same amount of work.\par \par \uc2 It seems unusual for the president and Congress to have this much say over labor disputes.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, the Railway Labor Act, which gives them this power, is an outlier. It goes all the way back to tumultuous strikes in 1877 that shut down rail service and prompted the president to send in troops. Because there are now alternatives to shipping by rail, like trucking, many labor experts argue that it's an outdated system that gives the railroads leverage over their work force. Rail workers can try to strike, but their only real play is to threaten to sabotage the American supply chain, to disrupt economic life for everyone.\par \par \uc2 As you noted, we now have trucks to move cargo. So how essential are trains today?\par \par \uc2 A hell of a lot of stuff still travels by rail -- 40 percent of freight in the U.S. And it's a central piece of the global supply chain. What we've learned through the great supply chain disruption during the pandemic is that if any part of that system slows down, we get backups everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 A strike would have produced a real economic shock. There would have been shortages of chlorine used in wastewater treatment plants and chemicals used to make paints and fertilizers. It would have meant higher prices for crops and other goods at a time when people are already paying more for groceries because of inflation. It would have disrupted jobs that depend on rail to move stuff, whether it's retail workers or contractors working on houses.\par \par \uc2 It's clear that the Biden administration recognized the political pitfalls of telling rail workers that the work they do is more important than the terms of their compensation. Biden was clearly spooked by the prospect of another supply chain crisis on his watch. And he opted not to force the railroads to swallow paid sick leave as the cost of averting a strike.\par \par \uc2 More about Peter: He's reported on supply chains for three decades and from more than three dozen countries. He's the author of '' Davos Man: How the Billionaires Devoured the World '' and is working on his next book, about pandemic shipping disruptions, called ''How the World Ran Out of Everything.''\par \par \uc2 NEWS\par \par \uc2 International\par \par \uc2 Cyril Ramaphosa\uc2 , South Africa's leader, refused to resign after a parliamentary report suggested he may have covered up a crime.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine called on civilians to evacuate a Russian-held area near the Dnipro River, signaling the start of a possible offensive.\par \par \uc2 A Ukrainian man was charged with treason after he returned a lost pilot to Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 Developing nations face a looming debt crisis, hit by rapid inflation and rising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 China's harsh pandemic restrictions have shaken citizens' trust in their government.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 Black voters in Georgia have mixed feelings about the Senate runoff between two African American candidates.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 FedEx\uc2  driver was charged with kidnapping and killing a 7-year-old girl in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Multiple gay men were robbed after dying of drug overdoses in New York, raising questions about whether they were targeted.\par \par \uc2 The unsolved murders of four University of Idaho students has left many dorm residents hesitant to return to campus.\par \par \uc2 A Texas public policy group is waging a national crusade against climate action.\par \par \uc2 Laundress recalled about eight million cleaning products over a bacteria risk.\par \par \uc2 WORLD CUP\par \par \uc2 Knockout: The U.S. team lost to the Netherlands 3-1, ending its World Cup run. Expectations will be higher for the young American team in four years.\par \par \uc2 Quarterfinals: Argentina beat Australia to advance thanks to an early goal from its star, Lionel Messi.\par \par \uc2 Today: France will play Poland at 10 a.m. Eastern and England will face off against Senegal at 2 p.m.\par \par \uc2 FROM OPINION\par \par \uc2 China faces a stark choice: Relax its Covid policies and risk a devastating outbreak, or clamp down and risk more unrest, Yanzhong Huang writes.\par \par \uc2 ''Free to Be ... You and Me,'' a treasury of songs, poems and stories, shaped a generation. Have we moved past its egalitarian ideals?, Pamela Paul asks.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian women are fighting to defend their country -- and to make it less sexist, Nicholas Kristof writes.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans who want to move on from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia offers an appealing vision of the future, Michelle Cottle writes.\par \par \uc2 The Sunday question: Which state should vote first?\par \par \uc2 As Democrats shake up their presidential primary lineup, dethroning Iowa -- a rural, white state that's no longer a national bellwether -- makes sense, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 's Jonathan Bernstein writes. In Politico, Lanae Erickson and David de la Fuente suggest moving up South Carolina and Nevada to reflect the party's diversity without slighting Iowa or New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Winter adventures: New hotels for skiing, snowshoeing and even snow-birding.\par \par \uc2 Rare emerald: A gem from a 1622 shipwreck is being auctioned.\par \par \uc2 Sunday routine: A World Cup podcaster expands her record collection.\par \par \uc2 Advice from Wirecutter: These are the best bags and backpacks.\par \par \uc2 Noodle: A pug famous on TikTok died at 14.\par \par \uc2 BOOKS\par \par \uc2 ''Butts: A Backstory'': Examining the posterior in cultural history.\par \par \uc2 By the Book: Prue Leith shares her favorite cookbooks.\par \par \uc2 Our editors' picks: ''Eat Your Mind,'' about the radical life of the author Kathy Acker, and 10 other books.\par \par \uc2 Times best sellers: Jerry Seinfeld's ''The Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee Book'' debuted as a hardcover nonfiction best seller. See all our lists here.\par \par \uc2 The Book Review podcast: Editors discuss the picks for top 10 books of the year.\par \par \uc2 THE SUNDAY TIMES MAGAZINE\par \par \uc2 On the cover: It's delivery season. But where does all the cardboard come from?\par \par \uc2 Recommendation: Join a community band.\par \par \uc2 Ethicist: She requested a Black mentee -- does she have to mentor a white law student?\par \par \uc2 Read the full issue.\par \par \uc2 THE WEEK AHEAD\par \par \uc2 What to Watch For\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court will hear arguments tomorrow in a case about whether a Colorado web designer can refuse working with same-sex couples.\par \par \uc2 Georgia holds its runoff election for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  on Tuesday between the Democratic incumbent Raphael Warnock and his Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wednesday is the 81st anniversary of the attack on Pearl Harbor.\par \par \uc2 Trevor Noah will host his final episode of ''The Daily Show'' on Thursday, ending a seven-year run.\par \par \uc2 The Heisman Trophy, awarded to the most outstanding player in college football, will be announced on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 What to Cook This Week\par \par \uc2 There's nothing wrong with eating dip for dinner, Emily Weinstein writes. One good option is Ali Slagle's grilled feta accessorized with a salad. Other weeknight ideas: turkey-ricotta meatballs or sheet-pan fish tikka.\par \par \uc2 NOW TIME TO PLAY\par \par \uc2 Here's a clue from the Sunday crossword:\par \par \uc2 33 Across: Flower cultivated by the Aztecs\par \par \uc2 Take the news quiz to see how well you followed the week's headlines.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Spelling Bee. Here's today's Wordle. After, use our bot to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your weekend with The Times.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Hard, Lauren Jackson, Tom Wright-Piersanti and Ashley Wu contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Fearing a renewed supply chain problem, Congress and the Biden administration imposed a labor agreement on rail workers and railroads. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DUSTIN CHAMBERS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221205eic500042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
