\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden Signs Bill Imposing Deal to Avert A Rail Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Zolan Kanno-Youngs and Emily Cochrane \par \uc2 661 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden had called on Congress to act, saying a rail strike would ''devastate our economy.''\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Biden signed legislation on Friday to impose a labor agreement between rail companies and workers who had been locked in a bitter dispute, averting a strike that could have upended the economy just before the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 ''Without freight rail, many U.S. industries would literally shut down,'' Mr. Biden said before signing the bill, adding that many communities would not have received crucial resources during the strike. ''Thanks to the bill Congress passed and what I'm about to sign, we spared the country that catastrophe.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden had called on Congress earlier this week to intervene in the stalemate and avoid the work stoppage that could have cost the economy $2 billion a day. It was a significant move for Mr. Biden, a staunch union backer who has previously argued against congressional intervention in railway labor disputes, arguing that it unfairly interferes with union bargaining efforts.\par \par \uc2 But he called for an exception in this case because a rail strike could have devastated the economy, snapping supply chains for commodities like lumber, coal and chemicals and delaying deliveries of automobiles and other goods.\par \par \uc2 The measure forces the companies and their workers to abide by a tentative agreement reached in September, which Mr. Biden helped to broker. That deal includes a 24 percent increase in wages over five years, more schedule flexibility and one additional paid day off. Several rail unions had rejected it because it lacked paid sick leave.\par \par \uc2 ''I know this was a tough vote for members of both parties,'' Mr. Biden said. ''It was tough for me. But it was the right thing to do at the moment. To save jobs.''\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats, under pressure from progressives to insist on the additional compensated time off for workers, tried and failed to push through a House-passed measure to add seven days of paid medical leave to the agreement. It was defeated 52 to 43, failing to secure the necessary 60 votes needed to pass and prompting multiple liberal senators to oppose the agreement altogether.\par \par \uc2 The Transportation Trades Department of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  criticized the members of Congress who did not vote for the House paid-leave measure.\par \par \uc2 ''Because of them, President Biden is not able to sign a resolution today that guarantees seven days of paid sick leave to all rail workers,'' said Greg Regan and Shari Semelsberger, the transportation union group's president and the secretary-treasurer, in a joint statement. ''While we are disappointed, we are not defeated.''\par \par \uc2 Congress acted under the Constitution's commerce clause, which allows it to regulate interstate commerce.\par \par \uc2 After signing the bill, Mr. Biden said he would work to secure paid sick leave for American workers ''before it's all over.'' Asked when rail workers can expect medical leave provisions, Mr. Biden said, ''as soon as I can convince our Republicans to see the light.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Mr. Biden scoffed at a question over why he helped broker an agreement without paid sick leave, saying that he had ''negotiated a contract no one else can negotiate.'' Last year, an initial social-spending package that failed to pass included a proposal for providing up to 12 weeks of paid sick leave.\par \par \uc2 The tension over the agreement was reflected in Congress in both parties. For Democrats, the failure to add seven days of paid leave for the workers stung. Senate Republicans tried to avoid intervening in the dispute altogether by implementing a cooling-off period of 60 days, but that proposal failed on a 70-25 vote.\par \par \uc2 ''I know this bill doesn't have paid sick leave that these rail workers and frankly every worker in America deserves,'' Mr. Biden said. ''But that fight isn't over.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221203eic30003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
