\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 17 Public Workers Are Charged With Stealing Covid Funds\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Weiser, Chelsia Rose Marcius and Jan Ransom \par \uc2 940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 They include \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2  workers and a jail official. The phony loans added up to more than $1.5 million, prosecutors said.\par \par \uc2 A New York City correction official, eight Police Department employees and eight other current and former city and state workers schemed to defraud Covid relief programs that were intended to provide money to struggling business owners, the authorities said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The defendants submitted phony applications for disaster relief loans on behalf of hair and nail salons and day care programs that did not exist, federal prosecutors in Manhattan said. The prosecutors said many defendants spent the proceeds of their loans on personal expenses, like casino gambling, stocks, furniture and luxury clothing.\par \par \uc2 They collectively stole more than $1.5 million from the federal \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  and financial institutions that issued guaranteed loans, and the intent was to steal hundreds of thousands of dollars more, the prosecutors said.\par \par \uc2 The defendants charged in the case also included employees of the city's Human Resources Administration, Department of Transportation, Administration for Children's Services and Department of Education as well as the state \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Scheming to steal government funds intended to help small businesses weather a national emergency is offensive,'' \uc2 Damian Williams\uc2 , the U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York, said in a statement. ''As public employees, these folks should have known better.''\par \par \uc2 The case is being prosecuted by the office's public corruption unit.\par \par \uc2 The Police Department employees included 911 dispatchers and a traffic enforcement agent; none were police officers. Other defendants include a bus operator and a social services eligibility specialist. The correction official, Edwin Skepple, was a 17-year veteran of his agency who holds the rank of captain.\par \par \uc2 Captain Skepple, 40, was arrested on Wednesday morning at his office on Rikers Island by investigators from the U.S. attorney's office, the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  and the S.B.A.'s Office of Inspector General, according to law enforcement officials.\par \par \uc2 Louis A. Molina, the Correction Department commissioner, said in a statement that Captain Skepplewould be suspended without pay.\par \par \uc2 ''It is extremely disappointing and inexcusable when a public servant violates the public's trust and chooses to commit fraudulent acts,'' Mr. Molina said. He said ''further action will be taken up to and including termination'' if Captain Skepple was found guilty.\par \par \uc2 Most defendants were taken before federal magistrate judges in the afternoon. Captain Skepple, in a brief hearing, was released on $100,000 personal bond pending further proceedings. His lawyer, Jonathan A. Marvinny, had no comment.\par \par \uc2 The charges unsealed on Wednesday are part of wave of fraud prosecutions around the country related to the trillions of dollars that the government has pumped into programs to bolster the economy and provide assistance to people who had lost their jobs. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported in August that the government had charged 1,500 people with defrauding programs that provide pandemic aid, with more than 450 convictions resulting from the cases. The \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2 's inspector general's office has agents going through two million potentially fraudulent loan applications.\par \par \uc2 According to criminal complaints filed by prosecutors, defendants submitted fraudulent applications for loans under the S.B.A.'s Paycheck Protection Program and the Economic Injury Disaster Loan program, which offered loans to help small businesses meet their financial obligations during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The loans ranged from $52,000 to $60,000, and the defendants often submitted applications on behalf of sole proprietorships in their own names and ''claimed six-figure gross revenues for businesses that actually earned far less, if they existed at all,'' prosecutors said. They listed employees they did not always have, according to authorities.\par \par \uc2 Many defendants conspired with a man whom prosecutors also charged to submit fraudulent applications and paid him kickbacks after their loans were funded, the government said.\par \par \uc2 In one case, a former Police Department 911 dispatcher received a $136,500 loan in July 2020 on behalf of an antiques and collectibles business called ''Thift'd N Gift'd.'' Another defendant who worked at the Administration for Children's Services applied for a loan in September 2020 for ''Neat Streaks Inc.,'' which was in the dry cleaners category, prosecutors said. That defendant had already received $49,000 that year from the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  in a separate fraudulent filing, according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 Captain Skepple, who was charged with one count of wire fraud, had a $50,000 loan approved by the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  in July 2020 thanks to a fraudulent application on behalf of a purported printing and graphic design firm, according to a criminal complaint.\par \par \uc2 In May 2020, he spoke with MyHouseDeals, a real estate investment website. In a post titled ''Rental Property Investing -- Edwin's Success Story,'' he said he was retiring from the Department of Correction in a few years and needed ways to supplement his income.\par \par \uc2 Captain Skepple said in the interview with the website that a broker had taught him the business, but he acknowledged making mistakes along the way, like hiring a property manager whose background he had not fully checked.\par \par \uc2 ''I just wanted to start bringing in the money and getting everything signed,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the impact of the pandemic on his investment efforts, Captain Skepple responded, ''I think the lenders are a little bit more free to lend.''\par \par \uc2 William K. Rashbaum and Brittany Kriegstein contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 William K. Rashbaum and Brittany Kriegstein contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221201eic100097\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
