\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Strikers at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  Seek Better Pay and Benefits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexandra Alter and Elizabeth A. Harris \par \uc2 741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees say they'll not return to work until they have a new contract. The company says it will ''continue to negotiate in good faith.''\par \par \uc2 Unionized employees at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  went on strike Thursday, saying they planned to stop working until they reached an agreement on a new contract.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  union represents about 250 employees in editorial, publicity, sales, marketing, legal and design. In a statement, the union said its members, who have been working without a contract since April, wanted better family leave benefits and higher pay.\par \par \uc2 Olga Brudastova, the president of Local 2110 of the U.A.W., which represents unionized \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  employees, said that the union had decided to go on an indefinite strike after negotiations with the company stalled. The union is proposing that \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  raise the minimum starting salary to $50,000, from $45,000. It has also demanded that the company address the lack of diversity in its work force.\par \par \uc2 Publishing has long been a low paying industry with long hours for its entry and midlevel employees, and it is based in New York, a very expensive city. It is also an overwhelmingly white industry, and many in the industry feel the low pay is part of what makes diversifying the industry difficult.\par \par \uc2 A recent report from PEN America, a free speech organization, said that according to company data, 74 percent of employees at \uc2 Penguin Random House\uc2  were white last of year, as were more than 70 percent of employees at Macmillan and about 65 percent of employees at Hachette. Those concentrations were even higher among senior managers.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, more than 100 union members marched and chanted in front of the \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  headquarters, some carrying signs that said ''Passion Doesn't Pay the Rent'' and ''Where the Wild Things Are Underpaid,'' a reference to a \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  classic by Maurice Sendak.\par \par \uc2 Some employees joining the strike said that they hoped their collective action would also drive changes across the industry.\par \par \uc2 ''This is bigger than our paychecks, this is about trying to make change in an industry that we think is broken,'' said Laura Harshberger, a senior production editor with \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  ' children's books division and the union chair, who was at the protest. ''This fight has really been focused on trying to make publishing a more diverse and equitable place that reflects our values and the books that we make.''\par \par \uc2 The union represents a small sliver of \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  's work force, which numbers around 4,000 globally.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  said that the company has already agreed to a number of the union's proposals, and that the company will ''continue to negotiate in good faith.''\par \par \uc2 In a memo about the strike that was sent to employees last week, \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  said that it had raised entry level wages and offered merit-based increases, and noted that it had ''implemented plans to ensure that operations continue uninterrupted during a potential strike.''\par \par \uc2 After posting strong, even record-breaking profits during the pandemic, many large publishers have seen their results start to slide. \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  announced this week that in its most recent quarter, its revenue was down 11 percent over the same period last year. Its earnings during that time fell further, down 54 percent to $39 million.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the publisher announced that it was laying off ''a small number'' of employees as part of a cost-cutting effort. The union filed an unfair labor practice charge with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  in response, Brudastova said, noting that six of the staff members who lost their jobs were union members.\par \par \uc2 Some authors, booksellers and agents took to social media to announce their support for the strike. Michelle Hauck, a literary agent, said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that she would not submit any new manuscripts to \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  until the strike is settled. Several authors who publish with \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  , including Micah Nemerever, author of the novel ''These Violent Delights,'' and Charish Reid, author of ''Mickey Chambers Shakes It Up,'' spoke out in support of the strike on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 One \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  debut author, Sarah Ruiz-Grossman, posted a message she had sent to the company in support of the strike: ''The books I love to read and write would not exist in the world without the essential work of these publishing workers,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221111eibb00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
