\par\par \uc2 CALCULATOR\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Best Cities for Remote Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Kolomatsky \par \uc2 369 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A study ranked large U.S. cities on how friendly they are to remote workers. (Forget about California!)\par \par \uc2 It looks like remote work is here to stay. Employers benefit by attracting talent from afar and saving money on office space, while workers save on commuting, office attire and, of course, housing. So where would you live if it could be anywhere?\par \par \uc2 A new study by LawnStarter, which scored the 200 largest U.S. cities on 20 metrics across seven categories to determine the best and worst places for remote work, could help you decide. One of those categories was earning potential, scored using each metro's median income. For that, head out west: The top five finishers were in California and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Despite its success in earning potential, though, California failed to place a city inside the top 90 of the study. In fact, the state's sky-high home prices dumped several of its metro areas into the bottom of the list.\par \par \uc2 The workspace category considered both at-home options (like the median number of rooms per home and the share of homes with one occupant) and temporary offices (like co-working spaces per capita). Helped by their high scores in these metrics, two Dallas suburbs, Plano and Frisco, Texas, topped the study overall. Frisco also scored well in the safety category, which was measured using crime statistics.\par \par \uc2 The amenities category awarded points for larger yards and convenient food delivery, while the connectivity category covered internet speed and coverage, and the affordability category rolled in the costs of living, rent and home prices, as well as taxes. Here, cities in Texas and Florida -- neither of which taxes personal income -- fared well.\par \par \uc2 The financial incentive category measured monetary benefits. Rochester, N.Y., for example, offers new residents $10,000 to move in and another $9,000 in home buyer incentives. Unfortunately, that wasn't enough for Rochester to place higher than 56th overall -- few cites offer such perks, and they had little effect on the overall rankings.\par \par \uc2 For weekly email updates on residential real estate news, sign up here.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page RE2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221023eian0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
