\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Sorting Out a Job Market That's Strong and Fluid All at Once\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Casselman \par \uc2 1,796 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The job market is still strong, but the long-expected slowdown has begun.\par \par \uc2 Keeping track of the U.S. economy can sometimes feel a bit like watching an action movie through a magnifying glass: lots of movement and flashing lights, but it's hard to figure out what's going on.\par \par \uc2 More often than not, the relevant data is a jumble of conflicting signals. Take the jobs report released on Friday. The unemployment rate fell, but the labor force shrank. Job gains slowed, but wage growth remained high. The report mostly met analysts' expectations, yet the stock market tumbled.\par \par \uc2 In times like these, it can help to put down the magnifying glass to look at the bigger picture. The bottom line: The job market is cooling, but it remains strong, and while a recession remains a real possibility, there's little sign of it yet in the data.\par \par \uc2 The story so far\par \par \uc2 Before we dive into the latest data, it's worth reflecting on just how far the job market has already come in the past two-plus years. The U.S. economy lost an astonishing 22 million jobs in the first two months of the pandemic. It has gained them all back, plus half a million more. After the previous recession, it took more than five years to achieve that feat.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic left a lasting mark on the U.S. economy. More people are working in warehouses today than in February 2020, and fewer in restaurants. Child care remains in short supply, forcing some parents -- a disproportionate share of them women -- to work part-time or not at all. ''Long Covid'' is also clearly keeping some people out of work, although researchers have come up with different estimates of how many.\par \par \uc2 But, for the most part, the labor market appears to have avoided the deep scarring that was the legacy of the last recession. Long-term unemployment is as low as it has been in 20 years. The overall unemployment rate hasn't been lower than this since the 1960s. Defying claims that ''no one wants to work anymore,'' 80.2 percent of Americans in their prime working years had jobs in September, above the rate in the year before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Getting there has been bumpy. As vaccines became widely available and businesses reopened last year, employers suddenly had more jobs to fill than applicants available to fill them. That was good news for workers, who were able to jump between jobs and negotiate for higher pay. But it almost certainly helped feed into inflation, as businesses raised prices to cover higher labor costs.\par \par \uc2 Where the economy stands\par \par \uc2 The labor market was always bound to cool off eventually. Almost no one thought that furious hiring pace was sustainable long-term. Now, the cool-down is clearly underway. Hiring is slowing. Job openings are falling. Fewer people are jumping ship to other employers.\par \par \uc2 But the job market is hardly nose-diving. Even though the total of 263,000 jobs added in September was the lowest in more than a year, it was still a healthy gain. Layoffs remain extremely low; fewer people are getting jobs, but we haven't seen any meaningful increase in the number of people losing them.\par \par \uc2 Again, it's easy to get lost in the details. Different measures of wage growth tell somewhat different stories. The same is true for job openings. And there are real and difficult questions about what is happening beneath the surface and why. But the basic story of a gradual slowdown is consistent across almost all the data we have available.\par \par \uc2 Two interpretations\par \par \uc2 Ordinarily, economists think of faster job growth as better. But the situation right now is more complicated. As my colleague German Lopez explained in this newsletter last week, the Fed has concluded that the job market is overheated and that the only way to bring down inflation is to cool it off. The question is how much pain it has to cause to get there.\par \par \uc2 There are two stories you can tell about the labor market right now. In the first, more optimistic, version, the gradual slowdown is akin to a healthy release of pressure. Employers have become a bit less eager to hire. Employees have become a bit less confident in their ability to demand raises. That will result in slower wage growth, which should allow the Fed to be more patient and less likely to slam the brakes on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The second version is darker. In this telling, the Fed has already been hitting the brakes pretty hard -- five rate increases this year, including three supersize hikes in a row -- and the job market has only just begun to slow down. Wage growth is still well above what the Fed considers consistent with its goal of 2 percent inflation. If policymakers want to get it down further -- and indications are that they do -- they will have to get even more aggressive, even at the risk of putting more people out of work.\par \par \uc2 For more\par \par \uc2 Retirees are about to get Social Security's biggest increase in decades. Here's what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 California's Inland Empire is a logistics hub for the supply chain. Some cities are fed up with the proliferation of warehouses.\par \par \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  and two others won the Nobel in economics for their research on banks and financial crises.\par \par \uc2 THE LATEST NEWS\par \par \uc2 War in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Explosions rocked Kyiv, Lviv and other Ukrainian cities this morning, killing at least five people in the most sustained missile assault in months.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians had been bracing for a retaliation from Moscow since an attack on Saturday that damaged a bridge linking Russia and Crimea.\par \par \uc2 Some Russian hawks say the strikes are a sign that the Kremlin is escalating the war.\par \par \uc2 Politics\par \par \uc2 A Supreme Court case could give state officials the power to overturn the popular vote in federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Some evangelical Christians continue to support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the Republican Senate candidate in Georgia who reportedly paid for an ex-girlfriend's abortion.\par \par \uc2 With less than a month to go, momentum keeps changing between Republicans and Democrats, making the midterms impossible to predict.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 A landslide in Venezuela killed 22 people. The country's vice president blamed the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 The Los Angeles City Council president is facing calls to resign after a leaked audio recording revealed she made racist remarks.\par \par \uc2 The San Antonio police fired a rookie officer after he shot and injured a teenager who was eating in a McDonald's parking lot.\par \par \uc2 California's bullet train is America's first experiment with high-speed rail, but political compromises made the project so expensive it may never be completed.\par \par \uc2 Kanye West, who goes by Ye, made antisemitic remarks on social media. Last week, he debuted a ''White Lives Matter'' shirt at Paris Fashion Week.\par \par \uc2 Opinions\par \par \uc2 Gail Collins and Bret Stephens discuss the midterms.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's efforts to stay out of the news -- and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's efforts to stay in it -- could save Democrats, Ezra Klein says.\par \par \uc2 Loretta Lynn embodied the South's complexities, Margaret Renkl writes.\par \par \uc2 Absent lasting improvements to Puerto Rico's infrastructure, storms will continue to devastate the island faster than it can recover, Yarimar Bonilla argues.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Picture of justice: Tracing Supreme Court history through more than a century of portraits.\par \par \uc2 Three-dimensional internet: This is life in the Metaverse.\par \par \uc2 Fake heiress: An interview with Anna Sorokin on her first night of house arrest.\par \par \uc2 Metropolitan diary: A public, one-man ballet recital.\par \par \uc2 Quiz time: Take our latest news quiz and share your score (the average was 9.4).\par \par \uc2 A Times classic: Tell your co-workers how much you make.\par \par \uc2 Lives Lived: Meredith Tax was a second-wave feminist whose scholarship on labor movements informed her own class-conscious activism. She died at 80.\par \par \uc2 SPORTS NEWS FROM THE ATHLETIC\par \par \uc2 Padres\uc2  eliminate Mets: The 101-win Mets are done after three playoff games in a Wild Card Series that saw everything backfire for them, including a foreign-substance check on the San Diego starter Joe Musgrove's ear in yesterday's loss.\par \par \uc2 A much-needed win: Justin Tucker hit a 43-yard field goal to give the Ravens a 19-17 win yesterday over Cincinnati and claim first place in the A.F.C. North. The win capped an intriguing weekend of football.\par \par \uc2 A suspension: The \uc2 Tampa Bay Lightning\uc2  suspended the defenseman Ian Cole after a woman accused him of sexually assaulting her when she was a minor. The team says Cole will be suspended until an investigation wraps up.\par \par \uc2 ARTS AND IDEAS\par \par \uc2 Nostalgic gaming\par \par \uc2 Some of this year's big video game releases are years, if not more than a decade, old. Blizzard recently rereleased World of Warcraft: Wrath of the Lich King, which first came out in 2008. The game's servers are so packed that some players have had to wait hours to sign on.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes the games are identical to their past versions; others get graphical and gameplay updates. More are on the way, including Resident Evil 4 and Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic.\par \par \uc2 The rereleases are part of a broader entertainment industry trend banking on nostalgia. As Imad Khan wrote in The Times: ''Nostalgia has always been a powerful source of revenue for Hollywood. Turns out, it's equally lucrative for video games.''\par \par \uc2 Related: Here's how to find old video games.\par \par \uc2 PLAY, WATCH, EAT\par \par \uc2 What to Cook\par \par \uc2 Sage pairs perfectly with these roasted potatoes.\par \par \uc2 What to Read\par \par \uc2 Colleen Hoover got her start self-publishing. This year, she has sold 8.6 million print books.\par \par \uc2 What to Watch\par \par \uc2 ''The Empress,'' a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series, examines the life of Elisabeth of Austria, who had a tattoo and drank wine with breakfast.\par \par \uc2 Now Time to Play\par \par \uc2 The pangrams from yesterday's Spelling Bee were aplenty, patently and penalty. Here is today's puzzle.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Mini Crossword, and a clue: Super (five letters).\par \par \uc2 And here's today's Wordle. After, use our bot to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your morning with The Times. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 P.S. David Leonhardt, this newsletter's lead writer, has started a book leave. Other Times journalists will be writing The Morning through late January.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's front page.\par \par \uc2 There's no new episode of ''The Daily.''\par \par \uc2 Matthew Cullen, Lauren Hard, Lauren Jackson, Claire Moses, Ian Prasad Philbrick, Tom Wright-Piersanti and Ashley Wu contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221011eiab00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
