\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 N.L.R.B. Backs Union In Dispute at \uc2 Activision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cade Metz \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The embattled video game company is in union negotiations with workers at its subsidiary Raven Software.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  said on Monday that \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2  had withheld raises from workers because of their union activity.\par \par \uc2 Activision\uc2 , the embattled video game company that has agreed to be acquired by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  in a $70 billion deal, withheld raises from quality assurance testers working for its subsidiary Raven Software, which helps test the Activision title Call of Duty, the board said. Workers there voted 19 to 3 to form a union in May.\par \par \uc2 The ruling, confirmed by a board spokeswoman, was reported earlier by \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision could give the new union additional bargaining power as it continues to negotiate with \uc2 Activision\uc2 . ''Unions are now trying to organize where they've previously feared to tread,'' said William Gould, a Stanford emeritus professor and a former N.L.R.B. chair.\par \par \uc2 If the two sides do not settle, the board will issue a complaint, which would result in a hearing with an administrative law judge, according to the board spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 In a statement sent to The Times, an \uc2 Activision\uc2  spokesman said that because of legal obligations under the National Labor Relations Act, it could not institute new pay raises at Raven. ''This rule that employers should not grant these kinds of wage increases has been the law for many years,'' the statement said.\par \par \uc2 More than 60 Raven employees walked out of their jobs in December to protest the company's decision to end the contracts of a dozen quality assurance workers. The next month, Raven employees said they were forming a union, a rarity in the North American video game industry. \uc2 Activision\uc2  declined to voluntarily recognize the union.\par \par \uc2 In April, \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2  said it was converting nearly 1,100 temporary workers at its studios to full-time status, but the minimum wage for the workers who are being hired did not apply to existing full-time quality assurance testers at Raven.\par \par \uc2 Activision\uc2  said at the time that it was barred from doing so because the \uc2 National Labor Relations\uc2  Act prohibited employers from inducing workers to vote against a union by increasing pay or benefits before an election.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after Raven employees formed their union, they filed a complaint with the N.L.R.B. claiming that \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2  discriminated and retaliated against quality assurance testers for their union activity.\par \par \uc2 Workers at an Activision subsidiary, Raven Software, filed a labor complaint after voting to form a union in the spring. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Mike Blake/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221006eia60002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
