\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 When \uc2 Disney\uc2 's Animators Went on Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Collins-Hughes \par \uc2 776 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cameron Darwin Bossert's smart new play fictionalizes a 1941 labor dispute to explore the tension between passions and paychecks.\par \par \uc2 On the lawn outside \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2 's snazzy new animation studio in Burbank, Calif., a young woman is out cold. The apple that was going to be her meager lunch has slipped from her grasp and rolled away.\par \par \uc2 It is 1941, her name is Betty Ann Dunbar, and her ambition is to become an animator -- though at \uc2 Disney\uc2 , being a female artist means having almost no chance of that. She works as a painter and inker instead, and if her salary is so measly that she can't afford to eat, so be it. She isn't living her dream, but she is living dream-adjacent, with work on films like ''Snow White'' and ''Fantasia.''\par \par \uc2 ''I mean, the stuff we get to make here,'' she gushes, after a worried colleague rouses her. ''I just love this place so much.''\par \par \uc2 But that's where you're vulnerable, isn't it, if the job you get undercompensated to do also fills your life with meaning. Throughout Cameron Darwin Bossert's smart and entertaining new play, ''Burbank'' -- a fictionalized retelling of a 1941 strike by \uc2 Disney\uc2  animators and the events leading up to it -- the tension between passion and paycheck thrums like an underscore.\par \par \uc2 In a spare, well-acted production by the company Thirdwing at the Wild Project, in Manhattan's East Village, Walt himself bestrides this lively drama, played by the author with a cigarette frequently in hand. On the cusp of 40, stymied by the war that's eaten into the European box-office prospects for ''Pinocchio,'' Walt views himself as benevolent, much the way he sees his cherished Mickey Mouse. Sure, Walt expects his people to work long hours -- the studio needs a smash ASAP -- but it's not like their environment is unpleasant.\par \par \uc2 ''Why the hell would anybody need to unionize at a place like this?'' he asks, as baffled as any 21st-century overlord who's provided every amenity to a captive staff. ''We got volleyball.''\par \par \uc2 Except that his employees' lives are falling apart. Not everyone blames Walt for that; Betty Ann (Kelley Lord) figures she can't afford to eat because she's single and bad at budgeting. But many \uc2 Disney\uc2  workers, like the animator Art Babbitt (Ryan Blackwell), want a union.\par \par \uc2 The creator of Goofy, Art is watching his marriage collapse because he's paid more attention to his drawings than to his wife, the dancer whose movements were a model for \uc2 Disney\uc2 's Snow White. And he is haunted by the fate of Adriana Caselotti, who voiced that same character in the studio's 1937 hit.\par \par \uc2 ''Adriana's contract stipulates that she cannot sing. Or act. In anything else. Ever again,'' Art says to Walt. ''Why would you do that to someone?''\par \par \uc2 The theme of taking a woman's voice is woven through this slender play, with its repeated mentions of ''The Little Mermaid,'' a fairy tale that has lately captured Walt's imagination. Online, Thirdwing puts the spotlight on female characters in ''Disney Girls,'' the ''teleplay series'' that's a streaming companion to the play. But ''Burbank,'' the second half of a diptych that started with ''The Fairest'' -- Bossert's 2021 play about the women of \uc2 Disney\uc2 's ink and paint department -- is primarily focused on Walt and Art.\par \par \uc2 Curiously for a piece whose characters are all deeply invested in visual art-making, it's in appearance that this production falls short -- not because it looks like it was made on a shoestring, which it does, but because the set and lighting design, which are uncredited, are underconsidered. The fake-grass mat standing in for the lawn is distractingly bad, while the lighting lacks the fluidity that the play's shifting moods and locations demand. But the period costumes, by Yolanda Bala\u241\'00\'F1a, are nicely done.\par \par \uc2 What's remarkable about ''Burbank,'' which does not have a credited director, is that while it's a labor drama, it sidesteps all of the traps that that phrase implies. Warm and alive, it's layered with nuance as it captures the anxiety that can grip a workplace amid a labor struggle -- and the ruthlessness that can ensue on all sides.\par \par \uc2 Burbank\par \par \uc2 Through Sept. 18 at the Wild Project, Manhattan; thirdwing.info. Running time: 1 hour 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Kelley Lord and Cameron Darwin Bossert in the Thirdwing company's presentation of ''Burbank,'' a fictionalized retelling of a 1941 strike. Bossert, the author, also plays Walt Disney. (PHOTOGRAPH BY VALERIE TERRANOVA) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220912ei9c0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
