\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Back-to-Office Turning Point\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Prasad Philbrick \par \uc2 1,800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Labor Day has become a flash point for big companies who want workers to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic upended American offices like nothing in memory. But now many big companies are again starting to require employees to work in person . To understand how that's going, I called my colleague Emma Goldberg, who covers workplace issues.\par \par \uc2 This subject is close to home for us: The Times expects employees to resume in-person work this month. Have you been in the office?\par \par \uc2 I've been going in about once a week. I love the energy of the newsroom, I love seeing people, and I miss my work friends. My beat feels like this intersection between the conversations I'm having with co-workers and the conversations I'm having with people in different states and industries. I get to follow the reporting threads that come from what my co-workers are experiencing. But I do get a lot done at home. And there are aspects of returning to the office that I'm apprehensive about, like having to commute instead of going on a morning run.\par \par \uc2 Some companies have tried to bring workers back, but Covid variants derailed those plans. Are those efforts ramping up again?\par \par \uc2 Many companies that planned to call people back into the office have done so, at least for a few days a week. After the Omicron wave, companies started saying, ''OK, we pushed it back, but we really want you to come back now.'' Labor Day 2022 has become a flash point for companies that have been in a hazy middle ground, with workers in the office one or two days a week.\par \par \uc2 Others have embraced permanent remote work. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 , for example, said its employees never need to come back to the office. Some have leaned into the idea that the office is going to be less of a space for work. \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  opened a kind of retreat center for nature walks, cooking classes and different activities that let workers spend time together. \uc2 Zillow\uc2 , which says it's ''headquartered in the cloud,'' has redesigned some of its office spaces to look more like social spaces.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about a minority of the work force that isn't back in person, right?\par \par \uc2 At the peak of lockdowns, in May 2020, just over a third of U.S. workers worked remotely at some point during the month, according to the Labor Department. Most blue-collar jobs never went remote, and many offices in small and midsize cities have since returned. But in the 10 largest U.S. cities, workers were still working from home around 38 percent of the time earlier this summer, according to researchers at Stanford and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Why do those workers continue to prefer remote work?\par \par \uc2 Covid still weighs on many, especially immunocompromised people or those with immunocompromised family members. For caretakers, remote work has meant more flexibility; previously, they might have felt pressure to wait until their boss left the office before scrambling to pick up their kids. Women and workers of color have been more likely to say they prefer to keep working remotely, sometimes attributing that to microaggressions they've experienced in the office. And some workers feel they're judged more on the quality of their work and not their capacity to office politick.\par \par \uc2 Why do companies want workers back?\par \par \uc2 That's the million-dollar question coming from workers right now, because it hasn't always been articulated. Some employers use squishy terms like ''company culture,'' or they talk about the serendipity of the water-cooler chat, the billion-dollar idea that comes from a run-in in the hallway. Other companies make the case that in-person interactions improve productivity. For many, it most likely comes down to leases. Commercial real estate leases are often very long, seven or even 10 years, especially for bigger companies. Some just have large spaces that they can't justify not using.\par \par \uc2 How are workers reacting to companies' return plans?\par \par \uc2 Many want at least some flexibility. They feel that remote work was proof that they can be productive even when they're not in the office. On the other hand, there's a real sense of missing work friendships: the person who sits next to you, whom you gossip with or vent to. And offices have really leaned into perks to get people back -- Ping-Pong, rock climbing, bands. \uc2 Google\uc2  even had Lizzo perform for its employees.\par \par \uc2 If anything is going to derail return-to-office plans, it will be workers feeling empowered to assert their needs, through unions or other groups. A lot of workers have developed a sense during the pandemic that work doesn't have to run their lives or come before their families or their health. \uc2 Apple\uc2 , for example, has said it wants employees back in the office at least part time after Labor Day. But a group of workers called \uc2 Apple\uc2  Together has put out several strongly worded open letters demanding to work remotely. And I've spoken to workers who have left companies and applied only for opportunities that let them be fully remote.\par \par \uc2 What will you be looking for as workers return?\par \par \uc2 The big questions are ''Will workers actually come back?'' and ''How far are managers willing to go to enforce their own rules?'' I'm also interested in the quirks of what returning will look like, from wardrobe malfunctions to commuting woes as people readjust to blazers and stilettos and subway rides.\par \par \uc2 More about Emma: She joined The Times in 2019 and last year published '' Life on the Line ,'' a book about doctors during the pandemic. Among her first introductions to office life was getting hit in the face during softball league as an intern at a Washington think tank. The person who broke her nose became one of her closest friends.\par \par \uc2 NEWS\par \par \uc2 The Latest\par \par \uc2 Chile will vote today on whether to adopt a new constitution that includes legal abortion, universal health care and rights to housing, internet, clean air and more.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  again postponed the launch of its moon rocket, this time because of a hydrogen leak.\par \par \uc2 Thousands turned out for \uc2 Mikhail Gorbachev\uc2 's funeral in Moscow. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  did not attend.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . has stationed two nuclear experts at the Zaporizhzhia power plant in Ukraine, hoping their presence will lower the risk of a catastrophic attack.\par \par \uc2 More than 14,000 UPPAbaby strollers are being recalled after a child's fingertip became caught in a brake.\par \par \uc2 A fast-moving wildfire in Northern California has destroyed at least 50 structures, including homes, and prompted the evacuation of thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 A new tool for crime: Ships are using fake satellite signals to evade sanctions and smuggle drugs.\par \par \uc2 Not long ago, the Republican Senate campaign committee was breaking financial records. Now, most of that money is gone.\par \par \uc2 In India, low-cost mopeds and rickshaw taxis are leading the transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 What did officials seize from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Mar-a-Lago estate? Here's an illustrated guide.\par \par \uc2 FROM OPINION\par \par \uc2 New Orleans's ''Katrina babies'' are resilient. They're also far from OK, Daut\u233\'00\'E9 Martin writes.\par \par \uc2 If President Biden really thinks Trumpian Republicans threaten democracy, he should be making policy concessions to win over parts of the right, says Ross Douthat.\par \par \uc2 Having trouble finding child care? You aren't alone, says Jessica Grose.\par \par \uc2 Pakistan emits little, but it's paying the price for climate change, says Fatima Bhutto.\par \par \uc2 The Sunday question: What legacy does \uc2 Mikhail Gorbachev\uc2  leave?\par \par \uc2 A peaceful end to the Cold War, freedom for former Soviet states and more liberty for Russians, says The Financial Times's editorial board. The Atlantic's \uc2 Anne Applebaum\uc2  thinks Gorbachev's failures to promote democracy, revitalize the economy and fully reckon with the Soviet Union's past wrongs helped birth today's autocratic and corrupt Russia.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Fanny Brice: Lea Michele landed her dream role in ''Funny Girl.'' The pressure is on.\par \par \uc2 Click: These websites want to make wedding planning feel more like online shopping.\par \par \uc2 Carr\u233\'00\'E9: Meet the man who amassed 10,000 vintage scarfs.\par \par \uc2 Sunday routine: How the editor in chief of Elle Decor spends his day.\par \par \uc2 Advice from Wirecutter: Consider a custom iPhone case.\par \par \uc2 A Times classic: The great Ivy League nude posture photo scandal.\par \par \uc2 BOOKS\par \par \uc2 By the Book: Mo Willems thinks you should reread the ''Peanuts'' collections.\par \par \uc2 Our editors' picks: ''Diary of a Misfit,'' a memoir that looks into a family mystery, and eight other books.\par \par \uc2 Times best sellers: R.F. Kuang's ''Babel'' is a No. 1 debut on our hardcover fiction list. See all our lists here.\par \par \uc2 THE SUNDAY TIMES MAGAZINE\par \par \uc2 On the cover: A Marvel role could make Jonathan Majors a superstar. He's so much more than that.\par \par \uc2 The Ethicist: What to do when an older acquaintance turns out to be a bigot.\par \par \uc2 It's good: Justin Tucker is the best kicker the N.F.L. has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 Talk: A conversation with Father Mike, the priest behind one of the biggest religion podcasts.\par \par \uc2 Read the full issue.\par \par \uc2 THE WEEK AHEAD\par \par \uc2 What to Watch For\par \par \uc2 Tennis's U.S. Open continues, with the quarterfinals starting Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  will preside over Mass Sunday for the beatification of John Paul I.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. will announce its new prime minister, chosen by Conservative Party members, on Monday. \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , the fervently pro-Brexit foreign secretary, is the front-runner.\par \par \uc2 Defense hearings are slated to begin Monday in the corruption trial of Argentina's vice president, \uc2 Cristina Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez de Kirchner\uc2 , days after a man tried to assassinate her.\par \par \uc2 The N.F.L. season opens on Thursday with the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  facing the \uc2 Los Angeles Rams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What to Cook This Week\par \par \uc2 At this time of year, Krysten Chambrot is looking for recipes that suit the temperatures (high) and her energy level (low). For weeknight dinners she recommends low-cook dishes including cheesy kimchi noodles, chicken and cabbage salad and sheet-pan feta with chickpeas and tomatoes.\par \par \uc2 NOW TIME TO PLAY\par \par \uc2 Here's a clue from the Sunday crossword:\par \par \uc2 10 Across: Cookout chuckouts\par \par \uc2 Take the news quiz to see how well you followed the week's headlines.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Spelling Bee. Here's today's Wordle. After, use our bot to get better.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your weekend with The Times.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Cullen, Tom Wright-Piersanti and Ashley Wu contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Labor Day 2022 was the moment for some U.S. companies to define their remote work limits. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AARON WOJACK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220907ei9700030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\p