\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 After an Early Climb, Market Extends Slump\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 684 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Stocks gave up an early rally and closed lower Friday, marking their third losing week in a row and extending Wall Street's late-summer slump.\par \par \uc2 Major stock indexes initially climbed broadly following the government's latest job market report, which showed employers slowed their hiring in August. The report put traders in a buying mood, stoking cautious optimism that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may not need to raise interest rates as aggressively in its ongoing bid to tame inflation.\par \par \uc2 But the market reversed course by midafternoon, shedding all its gains. That left the S&P 500 and Dow Jones industrial average 1.1% lower. The Nasdaq composite fell 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 ''The jobs report today was nice, but it was not enough to obviously sustain the rally,'' said \uc2 Ross Mayfield\uc2 , investment strategist at Baird. ''The bar to clear is 'does this change the trajectory of the Fed?' And I don't know that this report is enough to say yes.''\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the market has wiped out much of the gains it made in July and early August as traders worried that the Fed would not let up anytime soon on raising interest rates to bring down the highest inflation in decades.\par \par \uc2 The latest jobs data appeared to give traders some hope that a key driver of inflation is cooling. On Friday, the Labor Department reported that the U.S. economy added 315,000 jobs last month, down from 526,000 in July and below the average gain of the previous three months. The unemployment rate also rose to 3.7% from 3.5% in July.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly pay jumped 5.2% last month from a year earlier, but slowed slightly from July to August. That's a welcome sign in the inflation fight, as businesses typically pass the cost of higher wages on to their customers through higher prices.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's jobs report was a step in the right direction, in that the pace of job and wage growth stabilized,'' said \uc2 Matt Peron\uc2 , director of Research at Janus Henderson Investors. ''However, we reiterate our caution that we are not out of the woods just yet, as stubbornly high wage gains could keep the Fed on an aggressive path.''\par \par \uc2 The Fed has already raised interest rates four times this year and is expected to raise short-term rates by another 0.75 percentage points at its next meeting later this month, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2 . Following the jobs report, expectations for that three-quarter percentage point hike fell to 56% from 75% on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Market watchers such as David Kelly, chief global strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Asset Management\uc2 , said they still expect the central bank to raise rates later this month by another 0.75 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Signs of some slack in the labor market as well as more welcome news on falling gas prices ''increase the odds that the economy could gradually return to milder inflation over the course of the next year without falling into recession,'' Kelly said.\par \par \uc2 Stocks entered a skid last week after Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said the Fed needs to keep rates elevated enough ''for some time'' to slow the economy.\par \par \uc2 The latest jobs data comes a day after the Labor Department reported unemployment claims fell last week in another sign of a strong job market. It said earlier this week there were two jobs for every unemployed person in July.\par \par \uc2 The Fed will also get to review upcoming reports on consumer prices and inflation at the wholesale level, among other economic reports, before its next interest rate policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields, which have been rising along with expectations for higher interest rates, fell broadly. The yield on the 10-year Treasury, which influences interest rates on mortgages and other loans, slipped to 3.20% from 3.26% late Thursday. The two-year Treasury yield, which tends to track expectations for Fed action, fell to 3.40% from 3.52%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock markets will be closed Monday for the Labor Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220903ei930007j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
