\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Bosses Are Sharing Their Feelings. Their Workers Are Cringing.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg \par \uc2 1,902 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If you had told Ryan Caldbeck, in his first-ever job, that he would one day be crying to a room full of his employees, he might have simply told you -- with the bluster characteristic of any corporate striver -- that you were wrong.\par \par \uc2 But somewhere in between tumultuous markets and shifting national conversations on mental health, Mr. Caldbeck's perspective changed. In 2016, he laid off roughly 15 percent of his 45-person team at the financial technology company \uc2 CircleUp\uc2 , and broke down in tears. Now, he said, he shares his feelings with colleagues more openly. He also sometimes wrestles to find the line between buttoned-up executive reticence and oversharing.\par \par \uc2 ''There was a time when I went into work, and in a meeting we were going around the room saying how was your weekend, and I said, 'Oh, I got in a difficult fight with my wife,''' said Mr. Caldbeck, 43, who was previously \uc2 CircleUp\uc2 's chief executive. ''Everyone's mouth dropped. I realized I went too far. That wasn't appropriate. That's not how to show vulnerability at work.''\par \par \uc2 Chief executives have long studied how to deliver good news and bad news. They've trained themselves to pitch big ideas and shoot down middling ones. Now they have a new skill to learn: the art of being vulnerable. Emotional intelligence has landed atop the lists of best-selling management guides, like ''Dare to Lead,'' by Bren\u233\'00\'E9 Brown, whose books on vulnerability have sold well over a million copies. At the \uc2 Stanford Graduate School of Business\uc2 , the most popular elective course, for years, has been Interpersonal Dynamics, more commonly referred to as ''Touchy Feely.''\par \par \uc2 ''People in business are socialized to leave their feelings in the parking lot,'' said Carole Robin, co-author of ''Connect,'' who used to teach ''Touchy Feely.'' ''There's a generation of leaders now -- the ones that might be the really up-and-coming leaders of the future -- who have discovered that actually it's almost impossible to really inspire people in the absence of feelings.''\par \par \uc2 So, as companies navigate a challenging economic moment, executives are racing to let their employees know that they're not just empty suits. They're humans, with emotions, which they're sharing on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , in memoirs and in all-staff meetings. But their employees aren't always benefiting from the results of all that sharing. And, in some cases, they're feeling pressured to respond to their bosses by giving up their own privacy.\par \par \uc2 ''T.M.I. can lead to more vulnerability than you want,'' added Ms. Robin, who noted the distinction between appropriate and inappropriate workplace vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 Braden Wallake recently laid off two of his 17 employees at HyperSocial, the sales and marketing company he runs. He then went on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  to post his thoughts. ''This will be the most vulnerable thing I'll ever share,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wallake, 32, has always tended toward vulnerability, a leadership approach that he attributes to the podcaster Joe Rogan. He hosts a weekly ''wins call'' in which employees talk about triumphs in both their professional and their personal lives. He has traveled the country parking his van in some of his employees' driveways (including, several weeks ago, at the home of one of the workers he had just fired). When he started bawling, moments after laying off two workers, he couldn't imagine concealing that emotional response. He took a teary selfie for \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I just want people to see, that not every CEO out there is cold-hearted,'' Mr. Wallake wrote. ''I know it isn't professional to tell my employees that I love them. But from the bottom of my heart, I hope they know how much I do.''\par \par \uc2 The backlash was swift. The post generated more than 10,000 comments, many noting that Mr. Wallake had highlighted his own grief at a moment when his employees were suffering.\par \par \uc2 ''This 'you don't know how hard it is for me to fire you all' vulnerability is something people can stop doing, please,'' said Edward Sullivan, an executive coach and co-author of ''Leading With Heart.''\par \par \uc2 (One of the HyperSocial employees who were laid off, Noah Smith, said his \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  inbox had been flooded with interview offers after he defended Mr. Wallake's post. ''I'm only interested in working for people like Braden Wallake who has a positive outlook on life,'' Mr. Smith wrote.)\par \par \uc2 Recent years have seen business leaders trade old reliable corporate terms of art -- streamline, leverage, value add -- for words with a more human edge. Managers, encouraged by TED Talks and articles in Harvard Business Review, have started to talk about being authentic, transparent and vulnerable. That vocabulary expanded as many started working from home, interacting with their teammates through screens and desperately seeking moments of real connection.\par \par \uc2 But many workers have pointed out that when those emotional conversations go too far, they can create pressure to share what people would rather keep private.\par \par \uc2 ''In the old regime, it used to be that strength came from putting your emotions to the side,'' said Hitendra Wadhwa, a professor at \uc2 Columbia Business School\uc2  who teaches about personal leadership. He added that those previous misconceptions had given way to new ones: ''There are those struggling with the misunderstanding that strength comes from 'if I'm feeling this right now, I just have to express it.'''\par \par \uc2 Nancy Rothbard, a professor at the \uc2 Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania\uc2  who has studied emotions in the workplace, said her research pointed to a growing expectation that people bring their ''full selves'' to work, prompted by technologies that blur the lines between work and life. Surveys indicate that people tend to prefer segmenting their professional and personal lives instead of integrating them. But often they don't feel they have the option.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rothbard's research suggests that female and nonwhite leaders may face a steeper cost for emotional disclosures. One of her studies found that women are expected to share more personal information with colleagues, yet worry about whether it will be misinterpreted or used against them. Another found that people of color who share personal information with colleagues from different demographic backgrounds are more likely to find that experience challenging.\par \par \uc2 Her survey of more than 300 young Black, Hispanic and otherwise underrepresented professionals found that most prefer to open up to people from their own racial backgrounds rather than white colleagues -- yet they often work in predominantly white offices.\par \par \uc2 ''The psychological safety in terms of the group you're in makes a big difference,'' Ms. Rothbard said.\par \par \uc2 Julie Brosnan, who works in communications in Washington, recalled bracing herself for morning meetings with her boss at a previous job, when they would cover topics like: What'd you do last night? What'd you do last weekend? Then the boss would talk about her family fights, her husband and her affair.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Brosnan's managers referred to the staff as ''family.'' One worker's business was everybody's business. Ms. Brosnan was constantly fielding questions about her children, family life and how she spent her paid time off.\par \par \uc2 ''If I was on P.T.O., everyone would come up to me and be like, 'Where were you on Friday?''' she recalled. ''If I was like, 'I'm going to the dentist,' they'd be like, 'What'd you do at the dentist?' Like, I don't know!''\par \par \uc2 Some workers are choosing to shift their approach to workplace friendships, seeking to protect their privacy. Owen Paterline, 29, who works in radio, said he had typically been extroverted and open at work, just as he was in other parts of his life. But several months ago he looked around and realized that he didn't know any personal information about the peers he saw being promoted.\par \par \uc2 ''I was kind of like, 'Huh, I know this person is married, but I don't know anything about their marriage or kids,''' he said. ''I was like, 'Maybe I should stop saying things about how my dating life is going. Maybe I should be more buttoned up.'''\par \par \uc2 It's a delicate balance. Plenty of executives are relieved that the companies they run don't have the stifling environments they got accustomed to when starting their careers.\par \par \uc2 Andy Dunn\uc2 , for example, who co-founded the apparel company Bonobos and now runs Pumpkin Pie, a social app start-up, recently gathered his employees on a video call to tell them about his diagnosis of bipolar disorder, which is the subject of a book he released this year, ''Burn Rate.''\par \par \uc2 ''Once I got rolling, I kind of overshared,'' he said. He got off the call and immediately sent a \uc2 Slack\uc2  message to his chief technology officer asking if the conversation had been a ''disaster.''\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Dunn found that his own vulnerability prompted emotional disclosures from his employees. One person told him that she was feeling lonely in her current stage of life.\par \par \uc2 ''Would she be telling me those things in a different era? I don't know,'' Mr. Dunn reflected, adding that he feels his staff benefits from this openness.\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of the dating app Hinge, Justin McLeod, recalled the relief he felt in being completely straightforward with his staff when the company was re-evaluating its business model in 2016, facing stiff competition from Tinder. Mr. McLeod laid off half his staff, so he knew he couldn't protect the rest of the team from confronting the turbulence.\par \par \uc2 ''You can sugarcoat all you want, but it makes people feel less trustful of leadership,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 This summer's economic tumult has presented a new set of emotional challenges for business leaders. Ariane Goldman, founder of the start-up Hatch, which sells maternity clothes, has tried to strike a balance between protecting her team from her own anxieties while also being candid about downturns in the market.\par \par \uc2 ''It's really a positive thing for your team to understand where you're coming from,'' she said. ''I'm a founder C.E.O., not just a corporate gun for hire, so I'm bringing that baggage to the table with me.''\par \par \uc2 And with the labor market strong, some people are realizing that they can seek out the work environments that make them feel most comfortable, and leave boundary-breaking bosses behind.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Brosnan, for example, now has a group chat with friends who vent their frustrations about boundaries in the workplace. Some feel that their colleagues ask them to disclose too much; others feel strikingly distant from their teammates, especially when interacting with them mostly through video calls. Now Ms. Brosnan, who recently started a new job, has finally found a Goldilocks position.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no expectation that I would need to share pictures of my child, or talk about why I'm on P.T.O.,'' she said. ''We don't push it, but we're open and receptive to hearing about it if you're going through a hard time.''\par \par \uc2 ANDY DUNN, below, who runs Pumpkin Pie, a social app start-up, after telling his employees about his diagnosis of bipolar disorder. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LYNDON FRENCH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Braden Wallake, the chief executive of HyperSocial, posted this photograph of himself on LinkedIn after laying off two employees. (A15) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220825ei8p0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
