\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Why Gig Work Is Thriving\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lydia DePillis \par \uc2 1,682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Conventional employment opportunities abound, but online platforms still have appeal -- for flexibility or additional income.\par \par \uc2 American workers are experiencing, by many measures, one of the best job markets ever. The unemployment rate has matched a 53-year low. Job listings per available worker are at historic highs. Wages, while not quite keeping up with inflation, are rising at their fastest pace in decades.\par \par \uc2 So why would people keep doing gig work, a notoriously difficult and insecure way to make a living?\par \par \uc2 Online platforms like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  say the number of people providing services on their networks is rebounding steadily after a sharp decline early in the pandemic, while businesses like hotels and restaurants are breaking work into hour-by-hour increments available on demand.\par \par \uc2 Picking up shifts offers something that traditional permanent employment still generally doesn't: the ability to work when and as much as you want, demand permitting, which is often essential to balance life obligations like school or child care.\par \par \uc2 And lately, inflation has provided an extra incentive. As the cost of rent and food soars, gig work can supplement primary jobs that don't provide enough to live on or are otherwise unsatisfying.\par \par \uc2 Lexi Gervis, an executive at a financial management app called Steady, said that users' data showed that more people were involved in gig work -- and that the average gig income per worker grew -- from the start of the pandemic through this summer.\par \par \uc2 ''We were seeing this move towards multiple income streams, because that work was picked up as a stopgap and then continued,'' Dr. Gervis said.\par \par \uc2 Take Denae Bettis, a 23-year-old Steady user living in Severn, Md. After dropping out of college, she got a job at UPS, and after a few years rose to become a safety supervisor, usually starting at 4 a.m. During the pandemic, she took on more responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 ''The job got really stressful, and I felt like I had no way out,'' Ms. Bettis said. So in June 2020, she started a side gig through \uc2 Instacart\uc2 , shopping for people holed up at home. The next month, she quit her job, making it easier for her to pursue her passion: working as a personal makeup artist, which often requires taking early-morning appointments.\par \par \uc2 Surviving on income from gigs -- which for Ms. Bettis now include \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  as well as \uc2 Instacart\uc2  -- isn't easy. But Ms. Bettis thinks she can save enough money to open her own storefront.\par \par \uc2 ''We just went through a period where millions died, so are you going to spend your time at your job if it doesn't fulfill you?'' Ms. Bettis said, summing up gig work's appeal. ''Everybody loves stability, but if the flexibility isn't there, I don't think a lot of people are going to go back.''\par \par \uc2 Labor advocates have long been concerned about businesses that depend on independent contractors, since those workers aren't entitled to the rights and benefits that come with employee status, like employer contributions to payroll taxes and unemployment insurance. But while the model has gained traction, it has been difficult to pin down how fast the ranks of gig workers are growing.\par \par \uc2 The most accurate measure is \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  data on 1099 tax forms -- the freelancers' counterpart to the W-2 forms filed for employees -- but that is available only to select researchers and released with a lag of several years. At last count, in 2018, a team of economists found that about 1.2 percent of workers with any earnings had at least some income from online platform work. (A Pew survey from 2021 found that the share of all adults with gig income in a 12-month period was about 9 percent.)\par \par \uc2 The closest government metric that is more timely comes from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which asks people whether they count themselves as self-employed. That number rose significantly as a share of the labor force from early 2020 to early this year. But it generally captures people for whom self-employment is the main source of income -- which, for most gig workers, it isn't. More likely, the bump represents an increase in the number of people working as home improvement contractors and owner-operator truck drivers -- two longtime means of self-employment that surged during the pandemic -- and some white-collar freelancers.\par \par \uc2 Less comprehensive but more specific data comes from third-party platforms like Steady, which allows nearly six million workers to track their often-variable sources of income and posts incentives from gig platforms to try working for them. From February 2020 to June 2022, Steady recorded a 31 percent increase in the share of workers on the app with 1099 income. More of those were women than men, with particular growth among single mothers. Freelance income per gig worker increased 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the lines between gig work and traditional employment are blurring.\par \par \uc2 Staffing agencies have long supplied temporary workers for industries like warehousing and light manufacturing, where they would have to show up at a certain time on certain days until the business no longer needed the extra labor. Now, some agencies also offer one-off, no-commitment shifts in workplaces that rarely used temp labor before, like restaurants, hotels and retailers.\par \par \uc2 Under this approach, while offering the flexibility of gig work, the staffing agencies usually serve as the employer and administer benefits. Workers are paid as W-2 employees, not independent contractors, which means that they're still protected by federal labor laws and elements of the social safety net, including workers' compensation in the event of an injury.\par \par \uc2 Snagajob\uc2 , an hourly work platform, says that those shifts tripled from 2020 to 2021, and that they will probably quintuple in 2022 -- mostly as side income because people's regular jobs weren't sufficient.\par \par \uc2 ''I think if they were getting the ultimate flexibility and all the compensation they wanted from their full-time employer, there's probably less of a need for shifts,'' said \uc2 Snagajob\uc2 's chief executive, Mathieu Stevenson. ''But the reality is, at the overwhelming majority of businesses, you can't offer as much flexibility. So this is a way to say, 'If you do want to add an extra $150 because you need it, whether because you want to do something special with your family or you need to pay the light bill, this is an avenue.'''\par \par \uc2 More so than online gig jobs, it can also be a springboard to other opportunities.\par \par \uc2 It worked for Silvia Valladares, 24, who started picking up \uc2 Snagajob\uc2  shifts a few years ago to support herself as a college student studying fine arts in Richmond, Va., the company's initial market. Dishwashing and catering at different places allowed her to fit work in between her classes. But while working at an event venue called Dover Hall, she took a shine to hospitality, and decided to make that her career.\par \par \uc2 ''I got to know the regular staff and the management, and they got to know me,'' Ms. Valladares said. ''Eventually I asked if I could just work here, and they just put me on the regular staff.'' Now, as bed-and-breakfast director, she's the one posting gigs on \uc2 Snagajob\uc2  -- which lately have been filling quickly.\par \par \uc2 Worker advocates say allowing many competing employers to post last-minute shifts through an intermediary is probably a better model than a world of platforms that change rates at will and lack many of the legal obligations that employers must meet. But they say it still leaves workers on the margins of the labor market. Research on labor outsourcing has generally shown that temp workers are compensated less generously than co-workers who are hired directly.\par \par \uc2 ''You can look at it and say, 'This is great, people need jobs, these companies can do the matching, it's a win-win,''' said Daniel Schneider, a professor of public policy at \uc2 Harvard's Kennedy School of Government\uc2  who has studied low-wage work. ''The broader context is that it's really not. It's just a way for companies to shift costs and avoid economic responsibility.''\par \par \uc2 And while gig work has retained and even enhanced its appeal through the pandemic and recovery, it is not clear what will happen if the economy tips into recession and the number of conventional jobs starts to shrink.\par \par \uc2 Gig companies say it will bolster their labor supply, as the hardship caused by rising prices has. \uc2 Uber\uc2  said on its second-quarter earnings call that for 70 percent of its new drivers, the cost of living influenced their decision to join. ''There's no question that this operating environment is stronger for us,'' said \uc2 Dara Khosrowshahi\uc2 , the chief executive.\par \par \uc2 But in an economic downturn, an increase in worker availability for online platforms could coincide with a fall in demand. If customers reduce delivery orders and take fewer cab rides, it would be harder for those who depend on the apps to make a living.\par \par \uc2 That worries Willy Solis, a driver for the Target-owned delivery service Shipt in the Dallas area who has been an organizer for better conditions.\par \par \uc2 ''When people are desperate for work, that's usually what they want to do, is find something that's easily obtainable,'' he said. But what is good for the gig-work companies may not be good for the workers, he added. ''Whenever they do hiring sprees,'' he said, ''we see an influx in gig work and a decrease in the amount of work that's available to us.''\par \par \uc2 Denae Bettis of Severn, Md., supplements her income as a makeup artist by doing deliveries for DoorDash and Instacart. Above, a look at her workday on Aug. 2. (B1); Denae Bettis in Baltimore, above. She hopes that doing deliveries for gig-work apps will allow her to save enough money to open her own storefront as a makeup artist. Some of the products she uses, left. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ROSEM MORTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B5) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220816ei8g0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
